+# file; `--ensure` re-renders this one.
+
+# THE ONE COPY (2026-09-24). This reusable workflow is the org's only authored
+# copy of the Dependabot auto-merge logic. Every PRIVATE repo calls it through
+# a ~20-line caller (`scripts/dependabot_automerge.py` renders that caller and
+# checks every repo carries it); the templates render the same caller, so a
+# fix made HERE reaches the whole private fleet on its next Dependabot PR,
+# with no copier update and no per-repo PR.
+#
+# Why it moved here: the logic used to be copied into 40 repos and three
+# templates, and the copies drifted. Measured 2026-09-24, 40 of 41 repos still
+# merged with the GITHUB_TOKEN a day after the template fix below shipped (20
+# of them deploy_on_main), and 21 still lacked the 2026-09-06 `docker`
+# exclusion — each a strict older subset of the template, none with edits of
+# its own. Even the three templates had diverged (`fetch-metadata@v3` vs `@v2`).
+#
+# PUBLIC repos cannot call a workflow that lives in this PRIVATE repo, so they
+# carry an INLINE copy instead, which `scripts/dependabot_automerge.py` renders
+# from this file (same jobs, `pull_request` trigger) and checks byte-for-byte.
+# Edit this file, never an inline copy.
+#
+# Callers pass `secrets: inherit`. In a run started by dependabot[bot] the
+# caller's secrets are the org's DEPENDABOT store, and `inherit` forwards
+# exactly those — which is where CI_APP_* must live (see below).
+
 # Auto-merges low-risk Dependabot PRs once required CI checks pass:
 #   * any patch bump, and
 #   * any minor bump — direct production dependency or not,
@@ -34,6 +63,37 @@ name: dependabot-auto-merge
 # simplify this back to matching library semver — that reasoning is correct
 # for libraries and was silently over-applied to container tags.
 #
+# MERGING IS NOT SHIPPING — WHY THIS WORKFLOW MINTS AN APP TOKEN (2026-09-23).
+#
+# GitHub's documented recursion guard: an event caused by the `GITHUB_TOKEN`
+# starts NO workflow run. So when this workflow merged a Dependabot PR with the
+# default token, the resulting push to `main` produced **zero** runs of **any**
+# workflow -- not a failed deploy, no deploy. Measured 2026-09-19 on two live
+# `deploy_on_main` repos (`org-atlas-mcp`, `au-parliament-data`): both PRs
+# merged by `github-actions[bot]`, both `uv.lock` diffs matching the deploy's
+# own `paths:` filter exactly, production still serving the previous revision
+# days later while every signal read green. A token that is not the
+# `GITHUB_TOKEN` does not carry the guard, so the merge it performs builds.
+#
+# THE STEP IS INERT UNTIL THE SECRET EXISTS IN THE *DEPENDABOT* STORE, and that
+# is deliberate. A Dependabot-triggered run does not read Actions secrets -- the
+# runner prints `Secret source: Dependabot` (measured: org-atlas-mcp run
+# 35556905947). `secrets.CI_APP_ID` therefore resolves to `''` here unless the
+# org also holds it as a *Dependabot* secret, and the `if:` gate below makes
+# that case a skip with the old `GITHUB_TOKEN` behaviour, never a failure. The
+# `permissions:` block above IS honoured for Dependabot runs (same run:
+# `Contents: write`, `PullRequests: write`), which is why the merge itself has
+# always worked -- the token's *identity*, not its scope, is the defect.
+#
+# WHY THE MINTED TOKEN IS SCOPED TO ONE REPO. `repositories:` pins it to the
+# repo the run belongs to, so the credential this job can produce is never
+# estate-wide even though the App is installed org-wide. Note this does not
+# constrain someone who could exfiltrate the private key itself -- what makes
+# that unreachable here is that the job runs no PR-controlled code: it executes
+# `dependabot/fetch-metadata` and `gh`, never a project dependency, and no repo
+# in this org manages the `github-actions` ecosystem, so a Dependabot PR cannot
+# edit this file. Re-weigh this if either of those two facts ever changes.
+#
 # REQUIRES `allow_auto_merge: true` ON THE REPO. That setting defaults to FALSE on
 # every newly created GitHub repo, and `gh pr merge --auto` is a SILENT no-op without
 # it — the job still reports success, so the breakage is invisible. On 2026-07-27 it
@@ -52,10 +112,38 @@ jobs:
   auto-merge:
     if: ${{ github.actor == 'dependabot[bot]' }}
     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    # `secrets` is NOT a valid context in a step-level `if:`, so surface
+    # CI_APP_ID through a job-level env -- `env` IS valid there. Without this
+    # the workflow fails to compile rather than skipping.
+    env:
+      CI_APP_ID: ${{ secrets.CI_APP_ID }}
     steps:
+      - name: Mint stromy-ci App token
+        id: app-token
+        if: ${{ env.CI_APP_ID != '' }}
+        uses: actions/create-github-app-token@v3
+        with:
+          app-id: ${{ secrets.CI_APP_ID }}
+          private-key: ${{ secrets.CI_APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}
+          owner: ${{ github.repository_owner }}
+          repositories: ${{ github.event.repository.name }}
+          permission-contents: write
+          permission-pull-requests: write
+
+      # The fallback below is deliberate (no red, no poisoned PR), but it must
+      # never be SILENT again: a merge made with the GITHUB_TOKEN starts no
+      # workflow, so on a deploy_on_main repo it ships nothing. Say so where a
+      # reader of the run will see it.
+      - name: Warn — this merge will not trigger any workflow
+        if: ${{ env.CI_APP_ID == '' }}
+        run: |
+          msg="CI_APP_ID is not in this repo's Dependabot secret scope, so auto-merge falls back to the GITHUB_TOKEN and the merge will start NO workflow (no deploy, no post-merge CI). Fix: scripts/sync-ci-secret-scope.py in stromy-org."
+          echo "::warning::$msg"
+          echo "$msg" >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
+
       - name: Fetch Dependabot metadata
         id: meta
-        uses: dependabot/fetch-metadata@v2
+        uses: dependabot/fetch-metadata@v3
         with:
           github-token: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
 
